#
# macOS: the Cocoa plugin's Objective-C classes on the 32-bit runtime (the
# i386 port).
#
# The 32-bit (fragile) Objective-C runtime fixes each instance variable's
# offset when the class is compiled, from its @interface, and so accepts
# instance variables only there - not in the @implementation, not in a class
# extension - and synthesizes no property by itself. Qt 5.15 was written for
# the 64-bit runtime only.
#
#   - every class's variables move into its @interface, which for QNSView,
#     the application delegate, the file dialog delegate, the menus and the
#     accessibility element is in the header; the headers include or forward-
#     declare what those variables need;
#   - QNSWindow and QNSPanel share their member through the QNSWindowProtocol
#     mix-in in qnswindow.mm; it is declared in both @interfaces instead;
#   - the properties that relied on automatic synthesis (QNSView's cursor and
#     its previous superview and window, the dock menu, the tray delegate's
#     platform icon) get a variable and an @synthesize;
#   - a Metal layer is never offered on i386: Metal has no 32-bit slice, and
#     the i386 build does not link it.
#
# Nothing else changes in behaviour, on any architecture.
#
# Apply: git apply, from the Qt source root
